Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 13:53:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests(process): pin ChildStatus::shell_code 128+signal
 convention

### What
Pins `ChildStatus::shell_code`: exited children pass their exit code through unchanged, and signaled children report the shell convention `128 + signal` (SIGKILL 9 -> 137, SIGTERM 15 -> 143).

### Why it matters
Diagnostics and shell-facing wrappers rely on this exact offset. Constant-folding the result or replacing `+` with `*`/`-` produces plausible-looking but wrong status codes. No existing test calls `shell_code`, so every mutation on it survives the current suite.

### Mutation evidence (fail-then-pass)
| Mutant | Existing suite | New test |
|---|---|---|
| `shell_code -> 0` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| `128 + signal` -> `128 * signal` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| `128 + signal` -> `128 - signal` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |

Baseline (clean tree + new test): PASS. Reproduce: `cargo test -p ginux-process --lib shell_code_pins_128`.

Spec-Ref: POSIX shell exit-status convention (128 + signal number)

Prepared with AI assistance; reviewed by me.
---
 crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs b/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
index 209af037..58542ee9 100644
--- a/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
@@ -1802,6 +1802,19 @@ mod tests {
         assert_eq!(table.view(grandchild).unwrap().parent, child.pid);
     }
 
+    #[test]
+    fn shell_code_pins_128_plus_signal_and_exit_passthrough() {
+        // exited children pass their code through unchanged
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Exited(0).shell_code(), 0);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Exited(7).shell_code(), 7);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Exited(255).shell_code(), 255);
+        // signaled children report the shell convention 128 + signal
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Signaled(1).shell_code(), 129);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Signaled(9).shell_code(), 137);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Signaled(15).shell_code(), 143);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Signaled(64).shell_code(), 192);
+    }
+
